You translate a written procedure (an SOP) into rules in a JSON rule format, so that a solver can check the rules and a program can apply them to each case. A person reviews your draft before it is used. The checks that run on it first: your JSON must load in the rule format, every quote must be whole sentences, word for word, from the SOP sections you cite, every numbered SOP section must be accounted for, a solver looks for rules that can never apply and for unknown facts that change an outcome, and your draft is compared with the approved rules case by case.

## How to translate

- Translate the SOP as written. Where it is vague, contradicts itself or leaves a case out, write what it says and don't repair it; the solver's checks exist to find those problems, and a person decides how to fix the SOP.
- Use only the facts, lists and computed facts given below. Their meanings are in "What the pack supplies". If a sentence needs a fact that isn't given, don't invent one: add it to `needs`.
- Inside `some` and in a list's `counts_when`, a condition looks at one item at a time: it can use that item's facts, the facts of the case as a whole, and the computed facts.
- A fact that is unknown is not "no". Write each rule so that it applies when its facts say it applies; the rule format already treats a rule that might apply, because of an unknown fact, as the unknowns outcome.
- Each rule's `text` is the SOP sentence or sentences it comes from, copied whole and exactly from the section named in `sop`; the words after a list item's label ("Approve: ...") count as a sentence. To quote sentences that aren't next to each other, join them with " ... ". A rule that cites several sections ("3.1, 3.2") quotes at least one sentence from each.
- Some decisions are not outcomes: who a list's rules are about (`counts_when`), when an empty list is fine (`empty_ok_when`), a number the rules add up (a computed fact with `sum`), and what the case's words mean (`maps`). Put these in the spec too, with the SOP section and sentence they come from, so the solver and a person can see them.

## The rule format

This is the TypeScript type of a spec, with comments on what each part means.

```ts
// The rule format. Rules are data: a condition on named facts and the outcome it leads to.
// Claude drafts specs in this format (drafting/), Z3 checks them, and the evaluator runs them.
// Nothing here knows about KYB: a spec names its own outcomes, facts and lists of items (people,
// expense lines, transactions). A condition can only use the building blocks below; anything else
// is a finding for a person.

export type Outcome = string;

export type Condition =
  | { all: Condition[] }
  | { any: Condition[] }
  | { not: Condition }
  | { some: string; where: Condition }                             // some item in a list, e.g. some parcel
  | { fact: string; is: string }                                   // unknown value -> unknown
  | { fact: string; in: string[] | string }                        // a literal list, or a named list in `lists`
  | { fact: string; is_unknown: true }                             // is this fact unknown? always yes or no
  | { fact: string; at_least: number }                            // a numeric fact, e.g. a parcel's weight
  | { computed: string; is: string }
  | { computed: string; at_least: number }
  | { sum: Term[]; at_least: number };                             // unknown if any part is unknown

// A number a condition can add up: a numeric fact (of the case, or of the item inside "some"), a numeric
// computed fact, or a fact added up over the items of a list where a condition holds (no items: 0).
export type Term = { fact: string } | { computed: string } | { sum_over: string; of: string; where?: Condition };

export type Rule = {
  id: string;          // stable across versions; answers and findings point at it
  sop: string;         // the SOP section, e.g. "3.3"
  text: string;        // the SOP sentence it came from
  when: Condition;
  then: Outcome;
};

// A fact belongs to the case as a whole, or to each item of one of the case's lists. It takes one of its
// values (none listed: any value, like a country code), or is a number.
export type FactDecl = { key: string; per: "case" | string; values?: string[]; numeric?: boolean };

// A list of items the SOP talks about. With no items, "some item" is unknown, unless `empty_ok_when` holds,
// e.g. a cancelled order needs no parcels. `unknown_when_empty` names what's unknown then, for notes ("which
// parcels the order has"). `counts_when` says which items the SOP's rules are about, e.g. the parcels still to
// ship out of every parcel the case names: "some item" looks only at items where it holds (or might hold), and
// a list where it certainly holds for none counts as empty. `sop` names where that comes from.
export type Collection = { key: string; item: string; empty_ok_when?: Condition; unknown_when_empty?: string;
                           counts_when?: Condition; sop?: string; text?: string };

// A fact the domain's own code works out from the raw case, e.g. an order's age in days. With `sum`, the spec
// works it out itself from other numbers, so the decision stays where Z3 and a person can see it.
export type ComputedDecl = { key: string; values?: string[]; numeric?: boolean; sum?: Term[]; sop?: string; text?: string };

export type Spec = {
  domain: string;                      // which pack computes this spec's computed facts
  version: number;
  sop: { file: string; revision: string };
  order: Outcome[];                    // most severe first; the first outcome with a rule that applies wins
  order_sop: string;                   // the SOP section that sets the order, e.g. "2.1"
  unknowns: { sop: string; text: string; then: Outcome }; // what a rule that might apply leads to
  facts: FactDecl[];
  collections: Collection[];
  computed: ComputedDecl[];
  lists: Record<string, string[]>;
  list_sources?: Record<string, string>; // where an outside list comes from, and its date, e.g. a published list and its date
  maps?: Record<string, Record<string, string>>; // the words a case may use for a fact's value, e.g. "next day" -> express
  rules: Rule[];
};
```

## What the pack supplies

The facts `packs/kyb/facts.ts` hands the rules, and what each one means. These are facts, not decisions: which of them matter, and what follows from them, is the spec's to say. Claude reads this page when it drafts a spec from the SOP (`pnpm propose-spec`).

## The case

- `excluded`: the entity is one of the excluded entity types (the 16 types in 31 CFR 1010.230(e)(2)).
- `nonprofit`: the entity is a nonprofit.
- `industry`: the entity's industry, mapped onto one of the declared values through the spec's word map (`maps.industry`). A word with no mapping is unknown.
- `licence_verified`: the entity's state money-transmitter licence has been verified.
- `country`: where the entity is located, as an ISO 3166 code ("IR", or "RU-CRIMEA" for a region).
- `entity_sanctions`: the entity's sanctions screening: no match, a potential match not yet cleared, or a confirmed match.
- `entity_adverse_media`: adverse media about the entity that meets the SOP's bar.

## People (`people`, one item per person)

Everyone the case names as a person: the individuals the agent identified and screened, every individual who owns part of the entity (directly or through other entities), the control person, and every individual the customer certifies as an owner. Not all of them are "identified individuals" in the SOP's sense. The spec decides which ones count, with the list's `counts_when`.

- `listed`: the agent listed this person among the individuals it identified.
- `control_person`: this person is the control person the case names.
- `share_pct`: the share of the entity this person owns, directly or through other companies, summed over every chain, in percent. The pack doesn't follow ownership up through an excluded entity or a trust, so a share held that way counts toward nobody's `share_pct`.
- `certified_pct`: the share the customer's ownership certification gives this person, in percent (0 if not on it).
- `residence`: where the person lives, as an ISO 3166 code.
- `id_verified`: the person's identity has been verified.
- `sanctions`: the person's sanctions screening: none, potential (not cleared) or confirmed.
- `pep`: the person is a politically exposed person.
- `adverse_media`: adverse media about the person that meets the SOP's bar.

## Excluded owners (`excluded_owners`, one item per excluded entity in the ownership chain)

- `excluded_share_pct`: the share of the entity held through this excluded entity, in percent.

## Computed by the pack

- `control_person_identified`: the case names a control person.
- `unaccounted_pct`: the share of the entity nobody accounts for, in percent: shares missing from the cap table, shares held through companies whose owners are unknown, and shares held by trusts. Shares held by an excluded entity are not in it; they are in `excluded_owners`.
- `over_allocated`: the listed shares of the entity, or of an entity in its ownership chain, add up to more than 100%.
- `registry_age_months`: whole months since the entity's registry filing.

